00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:53.870
Could you tell us a little bit,
for people that don't know,

00:06:53.870 --> 00:06:56.840
people that are not, like Mark,
into video games that much.

00:06:56.840 --> 00:06:58.077
What is Improbable?

00:06:58.077 --> 00:06:58.910
Why is it important?

00:06:58.910 --> 00:06:59.900
What do you do?

00:06:59.900 --> 00:07:01.150
ROB WHITEHEAD: Yeah, sure.

00:07:01.150 --> 00:07:02.941
So it's probably worth
telling a little bit

00:07:02.941 --> 00:07:06.380
about this kind of story behind
where we got to Spatial OS.

00:07:06.380 --> 00:07:08.600
In the early days
hanging out with Herman,

00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:12.230
and we had this dream of
a truly living world video

00:07:12.230 --> 00:07:17.330
game, whereby traditional
games are constrained

00:07:17.330 --> 00:07:19.520
either in terms of
their scale or in terms

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:21.870
of the fidelity of the world.

00:07:21.870 --> 00:07:24.722
So you'll get a very large,
open-world games which

00:07:24.722 --> 00:07:25.930
are very limited interaction.

00:07:25.930 --> 00:07:32.090
Or you get very, very smaller
more high-fidelity worlds,

00:07:32.090 --> 00:07:33.830
like a game like Battlefield.

00:07:33.830 --> 00:07:36.533
And we wanted to build a game
that was a truly living world,

00:07:36.533 --> 00:07:39.100
where there were
millions of trees,

00:07:39.100 --> 00:07:41.780
or millions of
animals, monsters.

00:07:41.780 --> 00:07:44.450
And the players themselves
were completely incidental

00:07:44.450 --> 00:07:46.250
inside that world, and
it had huge amounts

00:07:46.250 --> 00:07:47.570
of physical fidelity.

00:07:47.570 --> 00:07:51.010
And we knew we'd never seen
games like that before.

00:07:51.010 --> 00:07:53.570
And we knew that
this necessarily

00:07:53.570 --> 00:07:55.940
would require us to come
up with a way of having

00:07:55.940 --> 00:07:58.100
multiple computers in the
cloud cooperate together

00:07:58.100 --> 00:08:00.391
to simulate the same space,
because the world was going

00:08:00.391 --> 00:08:02.480
to be too rich and too
large for a single machine

00:08:02.480 --> 00:08:04.520
to be able to deal with.

00:08:04.520 --> 00:08:08.045
And as we evolved, we moved from
our early prototypical ideas

00:08:08.045 --> 00:08:10.970
of building a game into building
this foundational simulation

00:08:10.970 --> 00:08:14.180
platform, which is
what we're doing now.

00:08:14.180 --> 00:08:15.560
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:08:15.560 --> 00:08:19.740
All right, so you've built this
platform called Spatial OS.

00:08:19.740 --> 00:08:22.310
What can you do inside of it?

00:08:22.310 --> 00:08:23.900
Is it just games?

00:08:23.900 --> 00:08:26.506
What's the possibilities?

00:08:26.506 --> 00:08:28.130
ROB WHITEHEAD: Spatial
OS is a platform

00:08:28.130 --> 00:08:29.670
for building simulated worlds.

00:08:29.670 --> 00:08:31.430
So what do I mean by that?

00:08:31.430 --> 00:08:33.830
Any problem you have which
involves a set of entities

00:08:33.830 --> 00:08:35.809
which are things,
and they interact

00:08:35.809 --> 00:08:39.100
in unexpected and complex
ways, and the scale

00:08:39.100 --> 00:08:41.600
of those interactions and the
fidelity of those interactions

00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:44.720
is so large that
you want to be using

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:47.080
the power of multiple
machines, Spatial OS nails.

00:08:47.080 --> 00:08:50.770
So if you're trying to simulate
a very huge game world,

00:08:50.770 --> 00:08:53.180
then you're going to have
lots of player entities,

00:08:53.180 --> 00:08:55.789
you're going to have monsters,
trees, inventory items.

00:08:55.789 --> 00:08:57.580
If you're simulating
a city, those entities

00:08:57.580 --> 00:09:01.930
might be cars, road segments,
traffic signals, pedestrians,

00:09:01.930 --> 00:09:02.690
buildings.

00:09:02.690 --> 00:09:04.898
And it really depends on
what domain you're modeling.

00:09:04.898 --> 00:09:07.070
And to get sort of
formal about it,

00:09:07.070 --> 00:09:09.250
it's in the realm of
complex systems modeling.

00:09:09.250 --> 00:09:10.780
It's modeling systems
where you have

00:09:10.780 --> 00:09:12.196
lots of little
things interacting,

00:09:12.196 --> 00:09:16.045
and then you get crazy emergent
consequences from that.

00:09:16.045 --> 00:09:16.920
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:20.426
So this is the Google
Cloud Platform podcast.

00:09:20.426 --> 00:09:22.800
Usually, we talk about how
you use Google Cloud Platform.

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:24.660
But as well as that,
I think it's worth

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:27.780
noting there's a partnership
here between Google Cloud

00:09:27.780 --> 00:09:29.392
Platform and Improbable as well.

00:09:29.392 --> 00:09:31.350
Do you want to talk a
bit about how that works,

00:09:31.350 --> 00:09:34.830
and what that provides
for developers?

00:09:34.830 --> 00:09:39.450
ROB WHITEHEAD: Yeah, I think
both Improbably and Google

00:09:39.450 --> 00:09:43.500
agree, [INAUDIBLE] Spatial OS
lets people build fundamentally

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:45.480
new kinds of game
experience with these very

00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:47.580
massive, simulated,
meaningful worlds,

00:09:47.580 --> 00:09:49.410
but also lets
smaller teams do so.

00:09:49.410 --> 00:09:52.500
And the idea of our
games innovation program

00:09:52.500 --> 00:09:55.140
is about enabling
game developers

00:09:55.140 --> 00:09:56.830
to get credits to
start developing

00:09:56.830 --> 00:10:00.099
with Spatial OS for free.

00:10:00.099 --> 00:10:01.890
And essentially have
credit, so that rather

00:10:01.890 --> 00:10:03.480
than when you're
developing your game,

00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:05.820
you have to pay for
the compute being used,

00:10:05.820 --> 00:10:07.920
you only have to pay for
the compute after launch.

00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:10.530
So it's about helping
you get up and running,

00:10:10.530 --> 00:10:12.720
and not be afraid of really
pushing the boundaries

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:14.219
in terms of what you can do.

00:10:14.219 --> 00:10:15.010
MARK MANDEL: Sweet.

00:10:15.010 --> 00:10:18.860
So GCP's partnering with you to
help power that whole program.

00:10:18.860 --> 00:10:20.090
ROB WHITEHEAD: Yeah, exactly.

00:10:20.090 --> 00:10:20.510
MARK MANDEL: Perfect.

00:10:20.510 --> 00:10:22.760
And if people are
interested in the program,

00:10:22.760 --> 00:10:26.060
or they want to build something
on Spatial OS for that program,

00:10:26.060 --> 00:10:28.790
is there any information
upcoming or available

00:10:28.790 --> 00:10:29.840
right now?

00:10:29.840 --> 00:10:30.380
ROB WHITEHEAD:
Yeah, so we're going

00:10:30.380 --> 00:10:32.720
to be announcing the specifics
of the program closer

00:10:32.720 --> 00:10:33.980
towards GDC.

00:10:33.980 --> 00:10:38.030
But right now, anybody who
signs up through SpatialOS.com

00:10:38.030 --> 00:10:40.160
will be eligible to
apply for the program.

00:10:40.160 --> 00:10:42.710
And so if anyone's
interested in going through,

00:10:42.710 --> 00:10:45.300
I'd recommend they
go to SpatialOS.com

00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:47.314
and go through those steps.

00:10:47.314 --> 00:10:48.230
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:10:48.230 --> 00:10:51.080
So I wanted to learn a
little bit about how--

00:10:51.080 --> 00:10:55.490
you said that there's the
name of the OS, Spatial OS.

00:10:55.490 --> 00:10:57.290
Is it really an OS?

00:10:57.290 --> 00:10:58.850
What does it look
to be a developer

00:10:58.850 --> 00:11:00.657
and to build something
on top of it?

00:11:00.657 --> 00:11:01.740
ROB WHITEHEAD: Yeah, sure.

00:11:01.740 --> 00:11:03.323
I mean, that's a
really good question.

00:11:03.323 --> 00:11:06.380
So Spatial OS is a kind
of [INAUDIBLE] distributed

00:11:06.380 --> 00:11:07.317
operating system.

00:11:07.317 --> 00:11:08.900
So when you have
traditional operating

00:11:08.900 --> 00:11:12.950
systems like Android, Windows,
they run on a single machine.

00:11:12.950 --> 00:11:14.900
Spatial OS is a
layer above that,

00:11:14.900 --> 00:11:16.970
which lets you take
multiple computers

00:11:16.970 --> 00:11:19.550
and consider them to
be one, big computer

00:11:19.550 --> 00:11:21.530
and present an abstraction
on top of that.

00:11:21.530 --> 00:11:24.770
So specifically, Spatial
OS runs on top of a core OS

00:11:24.770 --> 00:11:27.770
on a cluster of
machines in the cloud,

00:11:27.770 --> 00:11:29.690
and then gives you APIs
so you can treat it

00:11:29.690 --> 00:11:31.594
as almost one,
big supercomputer.

00:11:31.594 --> 00:11:32.510
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:11:32.510 --> 00:11:38.900
So it sounds like it's video
game-specific Kubernetes,

00:11:38.900 --> 00:11:40.010
almost?

00:11:40.010 --> 00:11:42.920
ROB WHITEHEAD: Yeah, I guess
the core abstraction is

00:11:42.920 --> 00:11:45.800
you look at traditional-- well,
it's a platform as a service.

00:11:45.800 --> 00:11:48.350
And you look at kind
of traditional problems

00:11:48.350 --> 00:11:49.430
as a service.

00:11:49.430 --> 00:11:52.820
The early days, we had tools
like Google App Engine, which

00:11:52.820 --> 00:11:54.860
are about a stateless
web application

00:11:54.860 --> 00:11:56.640
with some persistent
data back-end.

00:11:56.640 --> 00:11:58.681
And that was great for a
certain group of people.

00:11:58.681 --> 00:12:00.994
If you want to build a
[INAUDIBLE] mobile back-end,

00:12:00.994 --> 00:12:03.410
or you want to build a web
app, you can just nail that out

00:12:03.410 --> 00:12:04.730
really fast.

00:12:04.730 --> 00:12:07.010
And then you had more
flexible platforms

00:12:07.010 --> 00:12:08.450
like Google Cloud
Functions, which

00:12:08.450 --> 00:12:11.900
let you have these transient
pieces of computation

00:12:11.900 --> 00:12:15.640
acting and responding to
things like [INAUDIBLE] bus.

00:12:15.640 --> 00:12:18.219
And from our side, we
found there wasn't really

00:12:18.219 --> 00:12:19.760
a platform within
an abstraction that

00:12:19.760 --> 00:12:22.370
was really about creating
these large-scale simulated

00:12:22.370 --> 00:12:23.390
environments.

00:12:23.390 --> 00:12:26.280
And Spatial OS, we felt
like it filled that end.

00:12:26.280 --> 00:12:29.570
And so rather than thinking
about your clustered machines

00:12:29.570 --> 00:12:33.015
as a set of a bag of containers,
like with Kubernetes,

00:12:33.015 --> 00:12:34.640
you think about your
clustered machines

00:12:34.640 --> 00:12:36.190
as a three-dimensional space.

00:12:36.190 --> 00:12:37.910
And it's a pretty
crazy abstraction,

00:12:37.910 --> 00:12:39.030
but it actually works.

00:12:39.030 --> 00:12:41.660
So you think of your cluster as
a huge three-dimensional space.

00:12:41.660 --> 00:12:43.197
And inside of it,
you put entities.

00:12:43.197 --> 00:12:45.530
And those entities are the
things I talked about before.

00:12:45.530 --> 00:12:48.580
In your city simulation, they're
your vehicles, your roads,

00:12:48.580 --> 00:12:50.270
your traffic signals.

00:12:50.270 --> 00:12:53.210
In your video game, they're
players, monsters, loots--

00:12:53.210 --> 00:12:54.570
all that kind of stuff.

00:12:54.570 --> 00:12:56.690
And then under the hood,
we're partitioning up

00:12:56.690 --> 00:13:00.230
the data and computation of
that world across a cluster

00:13:00.230 --> 00:13:02.480
behind the scenes for you.

00:13:02.480 --> 00:13:05.900
MARK MANDEL: OK, so
if I'm a developer who

00:13:05.900 --> 00:13:08.510
wants to build
something on Spatial OS,

00:13:08.510 --> 00:13:10.730
what does that development
flow look like?

00:13:10.730 --> 00:13:13.105
I think you mentioned maybe
there are command line tools?

00:13:13.105 --> 00:13:15.350
Or how do I build
something from start

00:13:15.350 --> 00:13:17.630
to finish with, say, an
engine like maybe Unity

00:13:17.630 --> 00:13:18.400
or something else?

00:13:18.400 --> 00:13:19.650
ROB WHITEHEAD: Yeah, for sure.

00:13:19.650 --> 00:13:22.060
So it's worth saying
from our side,

00:13:22.060 --> 00:13:23.780
we really believe
in people taking

00:13:23.780 --> 00:13:25.405
the tools they're
already familiar with

00:13:25.405 --> 00:13:27.330
to use our technology.

00:13:27.330 --> 00:13:28.730
So let's say you're
traditionally

00:13:28.730 --> 00:13:32.270
used to building a dedicated
server-based multiplayer

00:13:32.270 --> 00:13:33.200
game in Unity.

00:13:33.200 --> 00:13:35.905
You'd have a Unity-dedicated
server probably built out,

00:13:35.905 --> 00:13:37.280
Linux running,
and you'd probably

00:13:37.280 --> 00:13:40.132
put it on some either dedicated
machine or on some VM.

00:13:40.132 --> 00:13:42.340
And you'll tell some clients,
go and connect to that.

00:13:42.340 --> 00:13:45.570
And so with Spatial OS,
you download our SDK,

00:13:45.570 --> 00:13:48.120
which is just a series
of command line tools,

00:13:48.120 --> 00:13:50.840
and web UIs, essentially.

00:13:50.840 --> 00:13:53.589
And you get plugins
which actually

00:13:53.589 --> 00:13:55.380
tie into whatever game
engine you're using.

00:13:55.380 --> 00:13:57.710
So whether you're using
Unreal, or using Unity,

00:13:57.710 --> 00:13:59.690
or you're using your
own game engine,

00:13:59.690 --> 00:14:03.100
this essentially handles your
connection into Spatial OS

00:14:03.100 --> 00:14:08.300
and handles taking the part of
the world which your engine is

00:14:08.300 --> 00:14:10.920
simulating and synchronizing
it with the broader world.

00:14:10.920 --> 00:14:13.966
And yeah, you pretty much
have business as usual.

00:14:13.966 --> 00:14:16.340
So, like I said, once you've
built your application using

00:14:16.340 --> 00:14:18.667
your game engine of
choice, you build that

00:14:18.667 --> 00:14:20.750
out into what we call an
assembly, which is almost

00:14:20.750 --> 00:14:22.220
like a distributed executable.

00:14:22.220 --> 00:14:24.152
It's this application
which you're

00:14:24.152 --> 00:14:26.110
going to push into our
platform, and it's going

00:14:26.110 --> 00:14:27.620
to run across lots of machines.

00:14:27.620 --> 00:14:30.010
And from that, you just use
a single CLI command, just

00:14:30.010 --> 00:14:31.014
spatial deploy.

00:14:31.014 --> 00:14:32.930
And it takes your built-out
Unity application,

00:14:32.930 --> 00:14:34.639
and it pushes it
into our platform.

00:14:34.639 --> 00:14:36.680
And then it carves out
those VMs and that cluster

00:14:36.680 --> 00:14:38.554
behind the scenes, and
then instantly, you've

00:14:38.554 --> 00:14:41.630
got a simulated world running
there up in the cloud.

00:14:41.630 --> 00:14:44.090
And the next step is you
can take a URL to that

00:14:44.090 --> 00:14:46.600
and share it with a friend,
and have both of you log in.

00:14:46.600 --> 00:14:48.141
MARK MANDEL: Cool,
but does that mean

00:14:48.141 --> 00:14:51.050
I have to write in some
sort of custom language?

00:14:51.050 --> 00:14:53.450
Am my escaping my
usual set of tools?

00:14:53.450 --> 00:14:54.530
How does that work?

00:14:54.530 --> 00:14:57.260
ROB WHITEHEAD: No, you can use
your existing set of tools,

00:14:57.260 --> 00:14:59.199
be it Unity,
Unreal, Game Engine,

00:14:59.199 --> 00:15:01.490
or whatever kind of tool you
want to be using for that.

00:15:01.490 --> 00:15:03.350
The thing which binds
this all together,

00:15:03.350 --> 00:15:05.630
and the kind of magic,
is traditionally,

00:15:05.630 --> 00:15:09.200
worlds would be built or run
from having a single dedicated

00:15:09.200 --> 00:15:10.400
server running in the cloud.

00:15:10.400 --> 00:15:11.858
Now, there's a
cluster of machines.

00:15:11.858 --> 00:15:13.890
So concretely, for a
game like Worlds Adrift,

00:15:13.890 --> 00:15:15.740
we're actually running
hundreds of instances

00:15:15.740 --> 00:15:19.580
of a game engine like Unity in
the cloud cooperating together

00:15:19.580 --> 00:15:23.030
to simulate a world much
bigger than any single one

00:15:23.030 --> 00:15:24.860
of those engines
could ever understand.

00:15:24.860 --> 00:15:26.235
And then plugging
into the world,

00:15:26.235 --> 00:15:29.660
as well, you can have
well over 1,000 clients

00:15:29.660 --> 00:15:31.670
interacting in the same
physical environment.

00:15:31.670 --> 00:15:34.364
And again, that's much
larger than a single game

00:15:34.364 --> 00:15:35.530
engine could ever deal with.

00:15:35.530 --> 00:15:38.710
And that's because Spatial OS is
a distributed cluster handling

00:15:38.710 --> 00:15:41.930
the in-memory persistence of all
those entities as they mutate,

00:15:41.930 --> 00:15:46.520
handling the network fan-out
of you moving around the world

00:15:46.520 --> 00:15:49.630
and having all the things
you're seeing streamed into you.

00:15:49.630 --> 00:15:54.720
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So going on the
developer experience of Spatial

00:15:54.720 --> 00:15:59.350
OS, I'm curious about if the
"Hello World" of Map Reduce

00:15:59.350 --> 00:16:04.382
is counting words, what is the
"Hello World" of Spatial OS?

00:16:04.382 --> 00:16:06.340
ROB WHITEHEAD: So if you
go through our GitHub,

00:16:06.340 --> 00:16:10.930
you'll find there's actually
an example, game simulation we

00:16:10.930 --> 00:16:12.100
call "Hello World."

00:16:12.100 --> 00:16:14.800
And it really is trying to
demonstrate the core aspects

00:16:14.800 --> 00:16:16.634
of what the technology does.

00:16:16.634 --> 00:16:17.800
So "Hello World," there is--

00:16:17.800 --> 00:16:21.685
I guess we coined this term,
[INAUDIBLE], which, I don't

00:16:21.685 --> 00:16:23.110
know if it's going to catch on.

00:16:23.110 --> 00:16:27.327
It was this idea of this "Hello
World" project has two teams,

00:16:27.327 --> 00:16:29.160
and the two teams are
running at each other,

00:16:29.160 --> 00:16:31.000
and these little wizards.

00:16:31.000 --> 00:16:33.910
And they start with very
small amounts of characters.

00:16:33.910 --> 00:16:35.500
And they build up,
and up, and up,

00:16:35.500 --> 00:16:38.680
until there are thousands a
side, attacking each other.

00:16:38.680 --> 00:16:42.880
And the cloud deployment has two
instances of Unity overlapping

00:16:42.880 --> 00:16:45.350
and co-simulating to
simulate the entire space.

00:16:45.350 --> 00:16:48.100
So it's a good example
of something showing

00:16:48.100 --> 00:16:51.850
an interesting game environment
with emerging complexity

00:16:51.850 --> 00:16:53.740
and interesting
gameplay mechanics.

00:16:53.740 --> 00:16:56.860
It's got the two workers
cooperating together,

00:16:56.860 --> 00:16:58.960
demonstrating that
Spatial OS is letting

00:16:58.960 --> 00:17:02.020
you use more than one machine.

00:17:02.020 --> 00:17:04.569
And yeah, that's the
foundational thing

00:17:04.569 --> 00:17:05.933
you tend to jump off from.

00:17:05.933 --> 00:17:06.849
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:17:06.849 --> 00:17:07.400
MARK MANDEL: I've played it.

00:17:07.400 --> 00:17:08.274
There's lots of fire.

00:17:08.274 --> 00:17:09.012
It's fun.

00:17:09.012 --> 00:17:10.720
ROB WHITEHEAD: Yeah,
Mark set me on fire.

00:17:10.720 --> 00:17:12.719
It wasn't very friendly.

00:17:12.719 --> 00:17:15.920
MARK MANDEL: That's
what friends are for.

00:17:15.920 --> 00:17:16.720
Cool.

00:17:16.720 --> 00:17:19.536
All right, so now
I'm also curious.

00:17:19.536 --> 00:17:20.740
So Spatial OS sounds great.

00:17:20.740 --> 00:17:24.430
Platform as a service for all
these massively online games.

00:17:24.430 --> 00:17:27.579
Why does Spatial OS, Improbable,
use Google Cloud Platform?

00:17:27.579 --> 00:17:30.227
What are you using
on us as products?

00:17:30.227 --> 00:17:31.310
ROB WHITEHEAD: Yeah, sure.

00:17:31.310 --> 00:17:34.590
So although as a developer,
you interact with Spatial OS,

00:17:34.590 --> 00:17:37.620
under the hood, with
the Innovation Program,

00:17:37.620 --> 00:17:42.180
we run on Google Compute Engine
to actually run those VMs.

00:17:42.180 --> 00:17:44.520
So when you create a
Spatial OS deployment,

00:17:44.520 --> 00:17:49.140
you're going to be using
Google VMs to get requisitioned

00:17:49.140 --> 00:17:49.950
and start.

00:17:49.950 --> 00:17:53.720
And the thing we
found with Google VMs

00:17:53.720 --> 00:17:56.370
is specifically around
their provisioning time.

00:17:56.370 --> 00:17:58.662
So say if you're a small
group of indie devs,

00:17:58.662 --> 00:18:00.120
and you're trying
to do a game jam,

00:18:00.120 --> 00:18:02.130
and you want to be iterating
rapidly and pushing

00:18:02.130 --> 00:18:05.886
your simulation to the cloud,
having a tight provisioning

00:18:05.886 --> 00:18:07.260
loop is really
good, because it's

00:18:07.260 --> 00:18:09.137
the amount of time you
go from a CLI command

00:18:09.137 --> 00:18:11.220
to actually having a game
running in the cloud you

00:18:11.220 --> 00:18:12.110
can go and play.

00:18:12.110 --> 00:18:16.870
And we found GCE was
specifically good for that.

00:18:16.870 --> 00:18:18.480
And the other side,
which is something

00:18:18.480 --> 00:18:21.450
to bear in mind with games
is a lot of the web is built

00:18:21.450 --> 00:18:24.120
around TCP, is built
around these cross-response

00:18:24.120 --> 00:18:26.130
connections, a lot of JSON.

00:18:26.130 --> 00:18:29.340
And when you move towards
applications like games,

00:18:29.340 --> 00:18:31.980
you're actually dealing with
firehoses of unreliable UDP

00:18:31.980 --> 00:18:32.850
packets.

00:18:32.850 --> 00:18:35.430
And traditionally,
a lot of clouds

00:18:35.430 --> 00:18:38.280
haven't really been set up to
be particularly good at routing

00:18:38.280 --> 00:18:39.450
that kind of traffic.

00:18:39.450 --> 00:18:43.140
And from our side, we
found Google Cloud Platform

00:18:43.140 --> 00:18:46.684
was a good choice in terms
of games networking as well.

00:18:46.684 --> 00:18:47.600
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:18:47.600 --> 00:18:52.220
So I think that on top of
using Google Cloud Platform,

00:18:52.220 --> 00:18:56.630
you might have also been
using other Google open source

00:18:56.630 --> 00:18:57.440
projects.

00:18:57.440 --> 00:18:59.660
You mentioned [INAUDIBLE],
and [INAUDIBLE]--

00:18:59.660 --> 00:19:02.600
I think their headline is
something like, "Google

00:19:02.600 --> 00:19:04.314
infrastructure for everyone."

00:19:04.314 --> 00:19:05.730
Are you using
something like GRPC?

00:19:08.332 --> 00:19:10.540
ROB WHITEHEAD: Yeah, there's
actually plenty of stuff

00:19:10.540 --> 00:19:11.790
to talk about.

00:19:11.790 --> 00:19:13.630
The first thing, yeah, is GRPC.

00:19:13.630 --> 00:19:15.940
Although the core
entity synchronization

00:19:15.940 --> 00:19:18.520
things within our tech
are kind of bespoke,

00:19:18.520 --> 00:19:21.580
the platform itself, in terms
of requisitioning deployments,

00:19:21.580 --> 00:19:24.670
getting metadata--
all that, we use GRPC.

00:19:24.670 --> 00:19:27.220
And the reason we
like that is-- one

00:19:27.220 --> 00:19:31.750
is very, very easy streaming
responses based upon HTTP2.

00:19:31.750 --> 00:19:35.290
And the other side as well
is having that kind of binary

00:19:35.290 --> 00:19:37.870
serialization by default.
And also having a typed RPC

00:19:37.870 --> 00:19:40.030
system by default
as well, rather than

00:19:40.030 --> 00:19:41.230
throwing around JSON.

00:19:41.230 --> 00:19:43.990
We found it was perfect
for our use cases.

00:19:43.990 --> 00:19:49.510
And I guess another thing in the
Google space we use is Basel.

00:19:49.510 --> 00:19:51.370
When you're building
a simulation platform,

00:19:51.370 --> 00:19:56.440
you're building APIs which can
be used in C#, C++, JavaScript,

00:19:56.440 --> 00:19:58.030
Java.

00:19:58.030 --> 00:20:01.430
And a tool like Basel lets
you handle a mono repo, which

00:20:01.430 --> 00:20:04.990
has a load of different
languages working together

00:20:04.990 --> 00:20:06.070
in the same build.

00:20:06.070 --> 00:20:08.260
So we found that invaluable
in terms of scaling

00:20:08.260 --> 00:20:09.552
as an engineering organization.

00:20:09.552 --> 00:20:11.343
MARK MANDEL: So, it's
actually interesting.

00:20:11.343 --> 00:20:13.570
Normally, when I hear
people using this many tools

00:20:13.570 --> 00:20:16.240
from Google open source
world especially,

00:20:16.240 --> 00:20:18.130
they're normally ex-Googlers.

00:20:18.130 --> 00:20:20.290
You're not ex-Googlers
though, right?

00:20:20.290 --> 00:20:23.260
ROB WHITEHEAD: So a few people
from our infrastructure team

00:20:23.260 --> 00:20:26.550
are ex-Googlers, and there's a
certain bit of that in there.

00:20:26.550 --> 00:20:28.600
We have a good cross-section
of different people.

00:20:28.600 --> 00:20:30.445
And a tech like
Spatial OS, you need

00:20:30.445 --> 00:20:32.080
to have this weird blend of--

00:20:32.080 --> 00:20:36.130
we have the ex-large-scale
software companies

00:20:36.130 --> 00:20:39.724
to give that production
and scale stuff.

00:20:39.724 --> 00:20:41.890
You've got people from
high-frequency trading firms,

00:20:41.890 --> 00:20:43.940
so they use that kind
of really low latency,

00:20:43.940 --> 00:20:45.247
high-throughput interactions.

00:20:45.247 --> 00:20:47.080
And you have people
from the gaming industry

00:20:47.080 --> 00:20:50.320
who are around all the
tricks and all of the skills

00:20:50.320 --> 00:20:52.510
which go into building a
very efficient game engine,

00:20:52.510 --> 00:20:53.105
for example.

00:20:53.105 --> 00:20:55.230
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So I guess
then, the next question

00:20:55.230 --> 00:20:57.706
is, are you hiring?

00:20:57.706 --> 00:20:58.830
ROB WHITEHEAD: Yes, we are.

00:20:58.830 --> 00:21:02.670
So we actually have
offices in San Francisco.

00:21:02.670 --> 00:21:04.670
We just set up recently there.

00:21:04.670 --> 00:21:08.410
We also have our home base
in Farringdon in London.

00:21:08.410 --> 00:21:10.720
So we have a lot of
us hanging out there.

00:21:10.720 --> 00:21:13.589
And yeah, we're always
looking for people

00:21:13.589 --> 00:21:15.630
who are interested in this
kind of thing in terms

00:21:15.630 --> 00:21:17.671
of building large-scale,
reliable infrastructure,

00:21:17.671 --> 00:21:19.650
and simulating the world.

00:21:19.650 --> 00:21:21.902
Or building the matrix.

00:21:21.902 --> 00:21:22.860
MARK MANDEL: Wonderful.

00:21:22.860 --> 00:21:25.580
So curious, though--
so you're building,

00:21:25.580 --> 00:21:27.470
it seems like, almost
a very different type

00:21:27.470 --> 00:21:29.979
of platform from what
people have built before.

00:21:29.979 --> 00:21:31.520
What were some
interesting challenges

00:21:31.520 --> 00:21:34.310
you faced when
building Spatial OS?

00:21:34.310 --> 00:21:35.810
ROB WHITEHEAD: Yeah.

00:21:35.810 --> 00:21:38.120
We had many, many
challenges on our quest.

00:21:38.120 --> 00:21:40.580
It's definitely,
definitely worth saying.

00:21:40.580 --> 00:21:43.650
Some fun stories to
talk about there.

00:21:43.650 --> 00:21:46.060
One is when you start running--

00:21:46.060 --> 00:21:48.800
normally a game will run
with a single server,

00:21:48.800 --> 00:21:50.260
binary running in the cloud.

00:21:50.260 --> 00:21:51.630
And occasionally, it'll crash.

00:21:51.630 --> 00:21:54.740
So the stuff is
high-performance C++ code.

00:21:54.740 --> 00:21:56.590
Sometimes, it's
going to have issues.

00:21:56.590 --> 00:21:59.960
Yeah, so traditionally, when you
run a single dedicated server,

00:21:59.960 --> 00:22:00.950
you'll find that.

00:22:00.950 --> 00:22:04.146
It's written in high-performance
C++ code, and occasionally,

00:22:04.146 --> 00:22:04.770
it might crash.

00:22:04.770 --> 00:22:06.800
But what happens when
you run 100 instances,

00:22:06.800 --> 00:22:09.870
or 1,000 instances of
that kind of game server

00:22:09.870 --> 00:22:13.155
code at the same time, as
a swarm of microservices?

00:22:13.155 --> 00:22:14.780
Statistically speaking,
the probability

00:22:14.780 --> 00:22:16.760
of any one of them
failing goes up and up.

00:22:16.760 --> 00:22:19.700
So we had to bake into
the core of our platform

00:22:19.700 --> 00:22:21.350
the fact that these
workers can fail.

00:22:21.350 --> 00:22:23.270
And how that works
is we actually

00:22:23.270 --> 00:22:25.610
have the in-memory
representation of the world.

00:22:25.610 --> 00:22:29.270
So every entity, every
player, rock, character

00:22:29.270 --> 00:22:31.710
has an in-memory representation
within the Spatial OS

00:22:31.710 --> 00:22:33.680
runtime of the state.

00:22:33.680 --> 00:22:35.990
And these workers are
almost streaming processes,

00:22:35.990 --> 00:22:40.949
that they're pulling in the
data of the world around it,

00:22:40.949 --> 00:22:42.740
simulating parts of
the world and streaming

00:22:42.740 --> 00:22:44.130
that back into Spatial OS.

00:22:44.130 --> 00:22:47.480
So at a moment's notice, if any
of those workers were to fail,

00:22:47.480 --> 00:22:50.162
we can rush in and
actually replace them

00:22:50.162 --> 00:22:52.370
with another worker who can
be given that information

00:22:52.370 --> 00:22:53.810
to continue the computation.

00:22:53.810 --> 00:22:57.170
So that's one
really fun problem.

00:22:57.170 --> 00:22:58.850
I say fun in retrospect.

00:22:58.850 --> 00:23:03.240
Obviously, they were incredibly
tough things to get through.

00:23:03.240 --> 00:23:06.020
So I guess one of the most
interesting challenges we

00:23:06.020 --> 00:23:09.560
had to solve is the fact
that physics engines, when

00:23:09.560 --> 00:23:11.090
you run a single
instance of them,

00:23:11.090 --> 00:23:12.644
they'll actually
use non-determinism

00:23:12.644 --> 00:23:14.810
in how they solve their
physics to actually increase

00:23:14.810 --> 00:23:16.640
the numerical stability
of the result.

00:23:16.640 --> 00:23:18.320
So you can imagine
a big pile of boxes

00:23:18.320 --> 00:23:22.179
being simulated by the
algorithms inside of them.

00:23:22.179 --> 00:23:23.720
By randomly shuffling
the order which

00:23:23.720 --> 00:23:25.790
those things are processed,
you end up with a better result

00:23:25.790 --> 00:23:26.780
mathematically.

00:23:26.780 --> 00:23:29.480
But when you start to have
1,000 instances of those physics

00:23:29.480 --> 00:23:31.724
engines overlapping and
cooperating together,

00:23:31.724 --> 00:23:33.140
you get these
really fun scenarios

00:23:33.140 --> 00:23:35.866
where you'll have overlap
regions, where both of them

00:23:35.866 --> 00:23:37.240
are interested in
the same world.

00:23:37.240 --> 00:23:39.910
And they might actually simulate
slightly different responses.

00:23:39.910 --> 00:23:42.440
So from that side, we
had to produce strategies

00:23:42.440 --> 00:23:45.860
and algorithms for essentially
blending their truths together

00:23:45.860 --> 00:23:47.984
after a set amount of time.

00:23:47.984 --> 00:23:49.775
And that was a really
interesting challenge

00:23:49.775 --> 00:23:53.210
where now, we can actually
stack a pile of boxes

00:23:53.210 --> 00:23:55.190
on top of each other
whilst migrating

00:23:55.190 --> 00:23:57.650
the authority of those boxes
between two physics engines

00:23:57.650 --> 00:24:00.500
multiple times a second, and
the physics simulation is still

00:24:00.500 --> 00:24:01.730
stable.

00:24:01.730 --> 00:24:05.090
So that's the way we think
about it is almost eventually

00:24:05.090 --> 00:24:07.520
consistent physical simulation.

00:24:07.520 --> 00:24:09.050
And like I said,
that was something

00:24:09.050 --> 00:24:12.733
we invested a lot of
effort in the early years.

00:24:12.733 --> 00:24:14.108
MARK MANDEL: My
head's exploding!

00:24:14.108 --> 00:24:14.324
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:24:14.324 --> 00:24:15.188
I could see.

00:24:15.188 --> 00:24:17.510
[LAUGHING]

00:24:17.510 --> 00:24:19.297
ROB WHITEHEAD: Is
that too crazy?

00:24:19.297 --> 00:24:21.130
MARK MANDEL: No, I'm
just thinking about it.

00:24:21.130 --> 00:24:22.200
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
It does make sense.

00:24:22.200 --> 00:24:23.616
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
it makes sense,

00:24:23.616 --> 00:24:25.400
but the non-determinism--

00:24:25.400 --> 00:24:28.010
ROB WHITEHEAD: I've got
another little cheeky soundbite

00:24:28.010 --> 00:24:30.121
thing I can say.

00:24:30.121 --> 00:24:32.120
The line of dominoes is
actually my favorite one

00:24:32.120 --> 00:24:33.245
to describe the technology.

00:24:33.245 --> 00:24:35.484
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, go on.

00:24:35.484 --> 00:24:37.400
ROB WHITEHEAD: I guess
one of the easiest ways

00:24:37.400 --> 00:24:41.150
to break down Spatial OS
into its most base example

00:24:41.150 --> 00:24:43.080
is a game which we're
not planning on making,

00:24:43.080 --> 00:24:45.740
but let's call it
Domino Simulator 2017.

00:24:45.740 --> 00:24:49.130
So in Domino Simulator 2017,
there are 1 million dominoes.

00:24:49.130 --> 00:24:51.650
And you can flick the
domino on the right side

00:24:51.650 --> 00:24:53.000
and have it cascade, cascade.

00:24:53.000 --> 00:24:55.770
And eventually, it'll knock over
the domino on the far left side

00:24:55.770 --> 00:24:57.390
a million down.

00:24:57.390 --> 00:24:59.390
Imagine you wanted to
simulate that in real-time

00:24:59.390 --> 00:25:02.510
and have people able to
participate and see that.

00:25:02.510 --> 00:25:04.442
The issue is, a single
physics engine wouldn't

00:25:04.442 --> 00:25:06.650
be able to simulate the
physics of a million dominoes

00:25:06.650 --> 00:25:08.120
at the same time.

00:25:08.120 --> 00:25:09.710
But we probably
have physics engines

00:25:09.710 --> 00:25:11.870
that could simulate
maybe 1,000, 10,000

00:25:11.870 --> 00:25:13.680
dominoes at the same time.

00:25:13.680 --> 00:25:17.330
So let's say we actually
bring up 1,000 physics engines

00:25:17.330 --> 00:25:19.970
all next to each other to
simulate that entire set of 1

00:25:19.970 --> 00:25:20.720
million dominoes.

00:25:20.720 --> 00:25:22.679
Well, you're going
to get an issue where

00:25:22.679 --> 00:25:24.470
when you flick the
domino on the far right,

00:25:24.470 --> 00:25:26.600
and it starts cascading
down, you get to the point

00:25:26.600 --> 00:25:31.390
where it needs to interact
with the next physics engine.

00:25:31.390 --> 00:25:34.800
And this is a very,
very difficult problem.

00:25:34.800 --> 00:25:36.350
And the way it works
is you can think

00:25:36.350 --> 00:25:39.800
of every entity, every domino
in the simulation to have

00:25:39.800 --> 00:25:41.300
almost a bubble around it.

00:25:41.300 --> 00:25:45.470
Imagine that, a bubble of half
a meter in real-life terms.

00:25:45.470 --> 00:25:47.690
And you can think
about, if I were

00:25:47.690 --> 00:25:51.950
to simulate the physics for
that single domino, what

00:25:51.950 --> 00:25:54.470
other things could actually act
on that at any point in time

00:25:54.470 --> 00:25:55.310
to effect it?

00:25:55.310 --> 00:25:57.440
Well, maybe the
dominoes very nearby it.

00:25:57.440 --> 00:25:59.694
But the domino a
million dominoes away

00:25:59.694 --> 00:26:01.610
might eventually affect
it, but it's not going

00:26:01.610 --> 00:26:03.200
to instantaneously affect it.

00:26:03.200 --> 00:26:05.750
And this idea of
bubbles of simulation

00:26:05.750 --> 00:26:08.630
and synchronizing the right
data to the right physics

00:26:08.630 --> 00:26:10.130
engines at the
right point in time

00:26:10.130 --> 00:26:13.090
is a lot of what the core hard
technical problems of Spatial

00:26:13.090 --> 00:26:14.217
OS are all about.

00:26:14.217 --> 00:26:14.495
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:26:14.495 --> 00:26:16.070
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That is pretty cool.

00:26:16.070 --> 00:26:20.210
So we were talking a little
bit before about "Hello World"

00:26:20.210 --> 00:26:22.600
and what are the
basic things that you

00:26:22.600 --> 00:26:24.430
can build with Spatial OS.

00:26:24.430 --> 00:26:26.500
What about the other
side of things?

00:26:26.500 --> 00:26:28.480
What is the biggest
game you know that has

00:26:28.480 --> 00:26:30.565
been built with Spatial OS?

00:26:30.565 --> 00:26:31.690
ROB WHITEHEAD: Interesting.

00:26:31.690 --> 00:26:33.130
So I can talk about games.

00:26:33.130 --> 00:26:34.630
The other thing I
want to talk about

00:26:34.630 --> 00:26:38.830
is some of the more
geographically explosive things

00:26:38.830 --> 00:26:40.164
that are non-games applications.

00:26:40.164 --> 00:26:41.246
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh, yeah.

00:26:41.246 --> 00:26:43.000
ROB WHITEHEAD: So
specifically one example

00:26:43.000 --> 00:26:45.850
is where somebody came in
for a week to jam with us,

00:26:45.850 --> 00:26:48.310
and they had knowledge in
internet network routing.

00:26:48.310 --> 00:26:50.080
So essentially,
that border gateway

00:26:50.080 --> 00:26:54.010
protocol, which is how all
the nodes of the internet

00:26:54.010 --> 00:26:55.750
find out how to read each other.

00:26:55.750 --> 00:26:59.380
And they simulated
every single node, just

00:26:59.380 --> 00:27:02.317
45,000 nodes in
the entire internet

00:27:02.317 --> 00:27:04.150
and could have a real-time
simulation of how

00:27:04.150 --> 00:27:05.450
those nodes reacted to failure.

00:27:05.450 --> 00:27:07.074
So you could be like,
what happens if I

00:27:07.074 --> 00:27:08.890
pull out this [? plow ?] cable?

00:27:08.890 --> 00:27:10.270
How will the internet react?

00:27:10.270 --> 00:27:13.849
And how will it move around its
routing of internet traffic?

00:27:13.849 --> 00:27:15.640
And that kind of thing
was really exciting.

00:27:15.640 --> 00:27:18.670
Seeing a small group
of people come in,

00:27:18.670 --> 00:27:22.360
they model some interactions
between two entities,

00:27:22.360 --> 00:27:24.520
the well-known set
of interactions.

00:27:24.520 --> 00:27:27.020
And then they could scale
it up to this massive scale.

00:27:27.020 --> 00:27:30.160
And I think that one of
the fun things about BGP

00:27:30.160 --> 00:27:32.917
is every single node has to know
about every single other node

00:27:32.917 --> 00:27:33.875
and how to route to it.

00:27:33.875 --> 00:27:38.950
So you get this really
fun, every single node

00:27:38.950 --> 00:27:41.560
has every other node's
routing information in it.

00:27:41.560 --> 00:27:45.526
So it grows with very,
very fun memory complexity.

00:27:45.526 --> 00:27:47.650
So it was interesting seeing
how much memory we had

00:27:47.650 --> 00:27:49.004
to consume to actually do that.

00:27:49.004 --> 00:27:50.920
MARK MANDEL: So that
sounds super interesting.

00:27:50.920 --> 00:27:53.830
Do you have any numbers you
could share in terms of how big

00:27:53.830 --> 00:27:54.720
or small that was?

00:27:54.720 --> 00:27:58.780
Or how many entities were being
used inside that simulation?

00:27:58.780 --> 00:28:01.090
ROB WHITEHEAD: Yeah, so with
our internet simulations,

00:28:01.090 --> 00:28:03.005
it's more about the
entities themselves

00:28:03.005 --> 00:28:04.630
being very heavy-weight
and doing a lot

00:28:04.630 --> 00:28:07.100
of algorithmic complexity.

00:28:07.100 --> 00:28:09.639
There are other simulations
in our city simulation

00:28:09.639 --> 00:28:11.680
space, which are in the
order of tens of millions

00:28:11.680 --> 00:28:15.130
of entities in terms of
representing buildings, roads,

00:28:15.130 --> 00:28:19.210
traffic segments,
vehicles, pedestrians,

00:28:19.210 --> 00:28:20.630
which are pretty exciting.

00:28:20.630 --> 00:28:22.990
And then in terms of our
gaming applications or games

00:28:22.990 --> 00:28:24.880
like Worlds Adrift,
which are using millions

00:28:24.880 --> 00:28:26.590
of entities interacting there.

00:28:26.590 --> 00:28:29.950
And they represent either from
a tree which you can chop,

00:28:29.950 --> 00:28:32.320
and it will physically fall
over and break into pieces,

00:28:32.320 --> 00:28:35.920
to creatures that fly around,
and they mate with each other

00:28:35.920 --> 00:28:38.400
and have a real
ecology around them,

00:28:38.400 --> 00:28:41.200
to players building ships
out of physical parts.

00:28:41.200 --> 00:28:44.560
So you can build a
floating sky ship out

00:28:44.560 --> 00:28:47.290
of hundreds of different
panels, and engines, and guns,

00:28:47.290 --> 00:28:48.280
and different pieces.

00:28:48.280 --> 00:28:49.330
And every single
one of those things

00:28:49.330 --> 00:28:50.590
is an entity which
is persistent.

00:28:50.590 --> 00:28:53.006
So if you crash into another
ship, and one of those panels

00:28:53.006 --> 00:28:54.590
falls over and
lands on the ground,

00:28:54.590 --> 00:28:56.256
that will actually
persist indefinitely,

00:28:56.256 --> 00:28:58.130
because it's stored
inside Spatial OS's

00:28:58.130 --> 00:29:00.400
in-memory representation,
which, again, is regularly

00:29:00.400 --> 00:29:02.170
snapshotted out.

00:29:02.170 --> 00:29:05.525
So even if someone goes away
and comes back again later,

00:29:05.525 --> 00:29:06.400
it'll still be there.

00:29:06.400 --> 00:29:08.440
Because rather than
having the transient stay

00:29:08.440 --> 00:29:10.231
inside of these game
engines, it's actually

00:29:10.231 --> 00:29:11.820
in a persistent layer on top.

00:29:11.820 --> 00:29:12.695
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:29:12.695 --> 00:29:14.800
All right, so we're slowly
running out of time.

00:29:14.800 --> 00:29:17.721
Where can people go if people
