00:31:42.487 --> 00:31:44.820
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, so I'll
ask the Question of the Week.

00:31:44.820 --> 00:31:47.180
So in Google Cloud Platform,
we have these things

00:31:47.180 --> 00:31:48.650
called persistent disks.

00:31:48.650 --> 00:31:51.920
We normally mount those
on virtual machines

00:31:51.920 --> 00:31:54.820
so that you can write files
to them, and put data in them,

00:31:54.820 --> 00:31:56.750
and all that sort of fun stuff.

00:31:56.750 --> 00:31:59.704
Sometimes, disks fill up,
and that tends to be bad.

00:31:59.704 --> 00:32:00.620
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:32:00.620 --> 00:32:02.495
MARK MANDEL: So one
option is to delete stuff

00:32:02.495 --> 00:32:04.490
off disk, which you may
or may not want to do.

00:32:04.490 --> 00:32:07.250
The other option is just
make the disk bigger.

00:32:07.250 --> 00:32:08.930
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yes.

00:32:08.930 --> 00:32:09.979
Or add more disks.

00:32:09.979 --> 00:32:11.270
MARK MANDEL: Or add more disks.

00:32:11.270 --> 00:32:13.386
But let's go with
making a disk bigger.

00:32:13.386 --> 00:32:14.510
I think that's just easier.

00:32:14.510 --> 00:32:17.300
Is there a way I can
make a disk bigger

00:32:17.300 --> 00:32:19.490
while not having to
take down my machine,

00:32:19.490 --> 00:32:21.390
or turn it off, or do
anything like that?

00:32:21.390 --> 00:32:23.840
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So this is
actually very interesting,

00:32:23.840 --> 00:32:28.100
because I think that the
first time I had this question

00:32:28.100 --> 00:32:32.390
was during a GCP 100 training.

00:32:32.390 --> 00:32:35.900
And I thought the
answer was, well,

00:32:35.900 --> 00:32:39.190
you can create a
snapshot of the disk,

00:32:39.190 --> 00:32:42.140
then create a new disk that is
bigger based on the snapshot

00:32:42.140 --> 00:32:44.782
so you have the same data,
and then swap the disks.

00:32:44.782 --> 00:32:46.490
MARK MANDEL: That
doesn't sound like fun.

00:32:46.490 --> 00:32:48.073
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I
mean, it is doable.

00:32:48.073 --> 00:32:50.582
But it turns out
that I was wrong.

00:32:50.582 --> 00:32:53.390
Or now, I am wrong, in any case.

00:32:53.390 --> 00:32:56.090
Because there's a way easier
way to do it, which is,

00:32:56.090 --> 00:32:58.310
you just go to the
console, and then

00:32:58.310 --> 00:33:01.820
you click on Edit on the
disk, and you resize it.

00:33:01.820 --> 00:33:04.190
No matter if it's attached
to an instance or not,

00:33:04.190 --> 00:33:08.030
you can actually do this, which
is an amazing thing to do.

00:33:08.030 --> 00:33:11.270
And then the second
part that you need to do

00:33:11.270 --> 00:33:14.690
is you actually need to let
the OS that you're running on

00:33:14.690 --> 00:33:18.440
that instance realize that
the disks changed its size.

00:33:18.440 --> 00:33:22.710
So you might need to change
the size of-- so for instance,

00:33:22.710 --> 00:33:27.260
if you're doing Linux, I think
there's a command called Resize

00:33:27.260 --> 00:33:29.054
to [? Fd-something? ?]

00:33:29.054 --> 00:33:30.470
MARK MANDEL: I
think that's right.

00:33:33.110 --> 00:33:37.040
FRANCESC CAMPOY: You can follow
the documentation for that one,

00:33:37.040 --> 00:33:39.500
because Resize to
[? Fd-something ?] might not be

00:33:39.500 --> 00:33:41.320
the actual accurate name.

00:33:41.320 --> 00:33:43.070
But basically, that's
what you need to do.

00:33:43.070 --> 00:33:45.570
You first resize
it from the console

00:33:45.570 --> 00:33:48.440
and, that is independent
for whatever system

00:33:48.440 --> 00:33:49.610
you might be running.

00:33:49.610 --> 00:33:51.950
And then according
to the system,

00:33:51.950 --> 00:33:54.080
you need to resize
that partition.

00:33:54.080 --> 00:33:54.710
And that's it.

00:33:54.710 --> 00:33:56.750
So you basically
are able to resize

00:33:56.750 --> 00:33:59.810
any disk with zero downtime.

00:33:59.810 --> 00:34:02.212
Except if the disk
is a root disk.

00:34:02.212 --> 00:34:03.920
If it is a root disk,
then you still need

00:34:03.920 --> 00:34:07.310
to go through that older process
that I was talking about,

00:34:07.310 --> 00:34:09.679
where you create first
a snapshot of the disk,

00:34:09.679 --> 00:34:12.290
and then you create a new
disk based on that snapshot.

00:34:12.290 --> 00:34:13.710
So you can still do it.

00:34:13.710 --> 00:34:15.000
It's a little bit harder.

00:34:15.000 --> 00:34:17.239
Which kind of makes
sense, because root disks

00:34:17.239 --> 00:34:18.519
are root and special.

00:34:18.519 --> 00:34:18.800
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:34:18.800 --> 00:34:20.591
And they have the
operating system on them,

00:34:20.591 --> 00:34:21.604
and other fun things.

00:34:21.604 --> 00:34:22.520
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:34:22.520 --> 00:34:25.330
So it is a little bit more
complicated to handle with.

00:34:25.330 --> 00:34:28.550
But, yeah, I think
it is a simple thing.

00:34:28.550 --> 00:34:31.096
And it's so simple
that sometimes, you

00:34:31.096 --> 00:34:32.929
